  "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(x,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x-2y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f08e4fc4d1d2933e9c12fe7df96a1029d66a83c68beff4fa31928cc010554766.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=2-x-2y\\,\u27f9\\,y=(2-x-z)/2.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x-z}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2639dbff9d1d4269abb59ccd3eefa0b246c8d1f183fd8f6ef132a41cc07e345d.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
